{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>If his offering is a burnt-offering of the cattle.</b> The burnt-offering precedes the other offerings in the section. By way of the plain sense (peshat): since thought is the beginning of everything, and the sin of thought precedes the sin of deed, therefore it was fitting to put the burnt-offering first. And the order Scripture sets for the burnt-offering is: first a bull, then a sheep, then a bird. A bull is the offering of a rich man, because his heart is coarse within him and he grows proud and trusts in his wealth, and therefore he was required to bring a bull; for a middling man, whose heart is not so coarse and who does not persist in sin like him, he was required to bring a sheep; and for a poor man, whose heart is submissive and lowly beyond other men, he was required to bring a bird, which is an insignificant thing. And so the offering of each and every one is according to his sin." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah, he put the burnt-offering first because it is Binah, and therefore the ordering of the sections corresponds to the bodies of the offerings: burnt-offering first, then peace-offerings, then the bull of the anointed priest, which is a sin-offering. And this corresponds to the order of the three Names that are among the thirteen attributes: the burnt-offering is above the attributes, the peace-offerings mediate among them in order to bring them to completion, [and] the bull of the anointed priest, which is the sin-offering, is for Knesset Israel, for thus it is written explicitly (Leviticus 16:30): ״וחטאתם לפני ה׳״."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:22:30.417829106Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}